    "sourceDiscrepancies": [
      {
        "problemId": "usaaao-2021-ii-second-exam-final-p6",
        "sourceNote": "The question defines speed u but writes beta=v/c. The key switches the photon distance from d_gamma to d_S and retains both v/u notations in the same derivation. Its printed gamma greater than 1 claim remains literal."
      },
      {
        "problemId": "usaaao-2021-ii-second-exam-final-p8",
        "sourceNote": "The original and repeated key question call both station and satellite masses m. The worked key uses m for the satellite and M for the station and assumes $m\\lt M$, a distinction absent from the original statement. The printed period chain ends with u/r; the energy paragraph changes the generic planet to Earth. Part (b) writes MV+mw=0 with V rather than radial W and changes an energy bound into an equality. All original notation, inequalities, labels and derivation steps remain literal."
      },
      {
        "problemId": "usaaao-2021-ii-second-exam-final-p9",
        "sourceNote": "The original key (c) first gives 0.076 helium nuclei but uses 0.075 in the following numerator; both printed values are preserved. In (h)iii the key accepts 13 as roughly consistent with 1, explains the exponential sensitivity, and gives 0.066 MeV as the exact solution. These original numerical claims and marking explanation are retained."
      },
      {
        "problemId": "usaaao-2021-ii-second-exam-final-p10",
        "sourceNote": "The key (c) prints 1/S in the solved alignment times, asserts alpha/beta/gamma are rational and immediately states they need not be rational, and refers to 2 n and m after the displayed 2 m−n counterexample. The entire original proof and two-page source Note are preserved without mathematical normalization. Part (f) repeats the tau 15= tauz sec 15 line twice, as printed."
      }
    ],
